artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the inverse problem of inferring the internal structure and thermal evolution of a giant icy planet (taken to be Uranus or Neptune) from remote and in-situ observations. The forward model maps an interior state — specified by radial profiles of density, composition (rock, water/ices, hydrogen–helium), temperature, and rotation, together with a thermal evolution history — to a vector of observables consisting of:

(i) the external gravitational field, represented by the zonal harmonic coefficients J_2, J_4, J_6, J_8, … obtained from spacecraft tracking (e.g., several Juno-like polar orbits);
(ii) the external magnetic field, represented by the Gauss coefficients of its spherical-harmonic expansion;
(iii) the atmospheric composition, in particular the abundances of He, H_2O, CH_4, NH_3 and noble gases, together with key isotopic ratios;
(iv) the bulk rotation rate (sidereal period) of the deep interior;
(v) the Bond albedo A_B; and
(vi) the wavelength-integrated thermal flux F_th emitted by the planet.

It is known that the inverse problem from gravity data alone (or from any single one of the above observables, however precisely measured) is highly degenerate: many distinct interior density/composition profiles reproduce the same J_{2n} to within current and foreseeable uncertainties.

Problem. Devise a quantitative joint-inversion framework that combines all six classes of observations above so as to break this degeneracy for Uranus and for Neptune. Specifically:

(a) Formulate a joint likelihood / Bayesian inverse problem in which interior-structure parameters and evolution parameters are simultaneously constrained by the gravity coefficients, magnetic-field coefficients, atmospheric abundances, rotation rate, Bond albedo, and thermal flux, with a precise specification of the forward operators and noise/observation models.
(b) Determine, for each observable, the measurement precision required so that the posterior on the key interior parameters (core mass and composition, ice-to-rock ratio, depth of the dynamo region, presence and location of compositional gradients, and luminosity history) becomes unimodal and identifiable.
(c) Prove (or give explicit numerical demonstrations of) the claim that no single observable, however accurate, suffices to render the inverse problem identifiable, while the combination above does.

Provide either a rigorous identifiability theorem for the combined inverse problem or an optimal experimental-design prescription that minimizes posterior uncertainty on the interior and evolution parameters of Uranus and Neptune.
